1
00:00:44,587 --> 00:00:47,715
Crow of Elysium, we honor thee!

2
00:00:47,798 --> 00:00:52,344
We honor thee!
We honor thee! We honor thee!

3
00:00:53,011 --> 00:00:56,181
No mortal flame can destroy common woe.

4
00:00:56,264 --> 00:00:58,767
Just one fire can conquer.

5
00:00:58,851 --> 00:01:02,605
Fire that burns in the
fields of Elysium.

6
00:01:02,688 --> 00:01:06,024
Hail, brothers! Many brothers!

7
00:01:07,067 --> 00:01:10,112
Brothers many, make brothers one.

8
00:01:10,195 --> 00:01:14,742
Hail, fellowship! Hail Crow
in his verdant kingdom.

9
00:01:14,825 --> 00:01:17,410
Common woe, be done!

10
00:01:18,536 --> 00:01:21,539
Hail, brothers! Common woe, be done!

11
00:01:21,624 --> 00:01:24,918
Set us free! Set us free!

12
00:01:25,002 --> 00:01:28,338
Set us free! Set us free! Set us free!

13
00:01:40,475 --> 00:01:44,146
Yeah... look around.

14
00:01:44,229 --> 00:01:46,165
Some of the most powerful men in the

15
00:01:46,177 --> 00:01:48,233
world are gathered in this clearing.

16
00:01:48,316 --> 00:01:52,237
Members of one of our country's
most exclusive clubs.

17
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:55,699
Invitation only, unless, of
course, you invite yourself.

18
00:01:55,783 --> 00:01:58,468
They gather here to
seek God between the

19
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:00,913
branches of these magnificent trees.

20
00:02:00,996 --> 00:02:03,874
They eat together, they
piss next to each other,

21
00:02:03,957 --> 00:02:06,669
and they carve up the world
like a Sunday roast.

22
00:02:06,752 --> 00:02:09,129
This is where the real power is.

23
00:02:09,212 --> 00:02:11,840
No women or presidents allowed.

24
00:02:11,924 --> 00:02:15,177
The former, I am not. The
latter, I will be again,

25
00:02:15,260 --> 00:02:17,966
because I have managed to reduce a

26
00:02:17,978 --> 00:02:21,016
national election down to one state.

27
00:02:21,099 --> 00:02:26,021
And while these men here can't swing
the votes of an entire nation...

28
00:02:26,104 --> 00:02:27,981
one measly state?

29
00:02:29,066 --> 00:02:33,320
Well, that they can do.
And that's why I'm here.

30
00:02:40,118 --> 00:02:42,579
So welcome to Elysian Fields.

31
00:04:38,445 --> 00:04:41,614
It's strange for you, isn't it?
Without him here.

32
00:04:42,866 --> 00:04:44,159
It's not strange, ma'am.

33
00:04:46,494 --> 00:04:49,372
LeAnn's in Cincinnati, trying to
close the deal for the debate.

34
00:04:49,456 --> 00:04:51,708
The president will make a series
of appearances across the state

35
00:04:51,792 --> 00:04:53,251
as soon as he gets back.

36
00:04:53,335 --> 00:04:56,463
If he survives the weekend. Anything?

37
00:04:56,546 --> 00:04:57,672
No phones allowed.

38
00:04:58,756 --> 00:05:00,508
Well, we're dividing and conquering.

39
00:05:00,592 --> 00:05:04,679
This Chinese trade agreement is going
to make Ohio voters very happy.

40
00:05:04,762 --> 00:05:05,889
It could help us win.

41
00:05:06,473 --> 00:05:08,766
- I didn't know the deal had closed.
- Well, we're on the verge.

42
00:05:09,935 --> 00:05:12,270
The president can make the announcement
in Canton when he appears.

43
00:05:12,354 --> 00:05:14,231
Oh, no, I'll be announcing myself...

44
00:05:14,314 --> 00:05:15,899
since I did the deal.

45
00:05:18,235 --> 00:05:20,278
Doug, you are very sincere...

46
00:05:20,946 --> 00:05:23,698
- and very loyal to Francis.
- And loyal to you, ma'am.

47
00:05:23,781 --> 00:05:25,158
I hope you know that.

48
00:05:26,159 --> 00:05:28,954
I don't mind it if you
call me Claire, you know.

49
00:05:30,747 --> 00:05:31,789
I'll show Ms. Davis in.

50
00:05:38,171 --> 00:05:39,839
Hi, Jane.

51
00:05:39,923 --> 00:05:41,827
Would you mind going over
these numbers with me

52
00:05:41,852 --> 00:05:43,200
before we meet with the minister?

53
00:05:43,260 --> 00:05:45,201
I have what China says they'll settle

54
00:05:45,213 --> 00:05:47,430
for, and what I know
they'll settle for.

55
00:05:47,514 --> 00:05:50,183
And listen, I want to thank you
so much for helping us with this.

56
00:05:50,267 --> 00:05:53,311
I'd like to see an
Underwood administration.

57
00:05:53,395 --> 00:05:54,897
Doug, that's all for now.

58
00:05:56,940 --> 00:05:58,150
Yes, ma'am.

59
00:06:24,467 --> 00:06:27,595
- You can show me.
- I don't want to.

60
00:06:27,679 --> 00:06:29,431
You don't have to protect me.

61
00:06:31,516 --> 00:06:34,186
Each of them has a story.

62
00:06:35,228 --> 00:06:37,480
Even if they got lost somewhere.

63
00:06:40,483 --> 00:06:43,153
It's harder to disappear
than you would think.

64
00:06:45,488 --> 00:06:46,531
But Rachel...

65
00:06:47,490 --> 00:06:48,783
is nowhere.

66
00:06:59,002 --> 00:07:01,296
I recognize that neck.

67
00:07:03,215 --> 00:07:05,925
Frank Underwood, as I live and breathe.

68
00:07:07,177 --> 00:07:10,555
Well, I owe you this.

69
00:07:10,638 --> 00:07:11,764
Oh.

70
00:07:12,432 --> 00:07:14,298
He didn't expect you to show up.

71
00:07:14,310 --> 00:07:16,519
I'm always happy to see old friends.

72
00:07:16,603 --> 00:07:18,688
And make new ones. Frank Underwood.

73
00:07:18,771 --> 00:07:22,025
- Benjamin Grant.
- Oh, of Pollyhop. Of course.

74
00:07:22,109 --> 00:07:24,194
You're a big supporter of my opponent.

75
00:07:24,277 --> 00:07:26,257
He's a forward-thinking
guy and a friend

76
00:07:26,282 --> 00:07:27,972
who should be president right now.

77
00:07:28,031 --> 00:07:30,617
There's no talking politics or
business while we're here, gentlemen.

78
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:33,286
Yes, we don't want to poison
all this nature with words.

79
00:07:33,311 --> 00:07:34,429
You know the motto.

80
00:07:34,454 --> 00:07:38,041
Yes, "Buzzing bees, sting not
here." It's hard to miss.

81
00:07:38,125 --> 00:07:39,601
Ted, I think you know everybody.

82
00:07:39,626 --> 00:07:43,005
Common woe never stood a chance.
Mr. President.

83
00:07:43,796 --> 00:07:45,507
First time you've been
invited, I understand.

84
00:07:45,590 --> 00:07:49,469
I'm almost sure there's an insult
in there somewhere, General.

85
00:07:49,552 --> 00:07:52,847
You know, Ted and I just had the
most remarkable piece of red meat

86
00:07:52,930 --> 00:07:54,975
at a steak house out in Canton.

87
00:07:55,058 --> 00:07:57,727
I can't remember, do you like prime rib?

88
00:07:57,810 --> 00:08:00,522
- In the right company.
- Uh-huh.

89
00:08:01,648 --> 00:08:02,649
If you'll excuse us,

90
00:08:02,732 --> 00:08:04,574
Ted and I promised young Benjamin

91
00:08:04,586 --> 00:08:06,778
here we'd find him an adult beverage.

92
00:08:06,861 --> 00:08:09,364
- Drink?
- I'd rather not.

93
00:08:12,909 --> 00:08:14,686
You know, everybody was looking forward

94
00:08:14,698 --> 00:08:16,579
to spending some time with the general.

95
00:08:16,663 --> 00:08:18,248
And now you're here as well.

96
00:08:19,541 --> 00:08:21,043
After this weekend, who knows?

97
00:08:21,126 --> 00:08:24,629
Maybe this goddamn bastard of an
election will get itself resolved.

98
00:08:26,131 --> 00:08:28,050
Oh, one little piece of advice

99
00:08:28,133 --> 00:08:30,218
that I could give you as
long as you're out here.

100
00:08:31,136 --> 00:08:34,764
If you don't bother the yellow
jackets, they won't bother you.

101
00:08:41,563 --> 00:08:43,690
So why didn't you invite Conway?

102
00:08:44,607 --> 00:08:47,194
Brockhart was invited instead.

103
00:08:47,277 --> 00:08:49,821
So there's not going to be a
debate next week, is there?

104
00:08:49,904 --> 00:08:51,531
Not a presidential one, no.

105
00:08:53,325 --> 00:08:55,452
I should really be in
Ohio, shaking hands.

106
00:08:55,535 --> 00:08:57,370
Yeah, you bargained to
get in here, Francis,

107
00:08:57,454 --> 00:08:58,996
and now you're here. So
let me suggest something.

108
00:08:59,081 --> 00:09:00,207
Fuck Ohio.

109
00:09:01,291 --> 00:09:03,043
This is the campaign.

110
00:09:04,669 --> 00:09:07,672
The general is a traitor, you know that.

111
00:09:07,755 --> 00:09:09,841
The general is trying
to be a politician.

112
00:09:12,052 --> 00:09:13,761
You would have done the same.

113
00:09:16,223 --> 00:09:17,515
Yeah, but better.

114
00:09:19,976 --> 00:09:22,145
<i>President Underwood?</i>

115
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:25,151
President Underwood? I'm sorry, ma'am,

116
00:09:25,163 --> 00:09:27,024
but you're needed in
the Situation Room.

117
00:09:36,493 --> 00:09:37,702
Cathy.

118
00:09:37,785 --> 00:09:41,081
I wanted a moment with you before
we join the rest of the team.

119
00:09:41,164 --> 00:09:43,708
- Oh, I was briefed on the way over.
- Not fully.

120
00:09:43,791 --> 00:09:46,128
A Russian research vessel
is stuck and sinking...

121
00:09:46,211 --> 00:09:49,089
Yes, they claim they were
taking core samples,

122
00:09:49,172 --> 00:09:50,923
some global warming nonsense.

123
00:09:51,007 --> 00:09:52,925
But they were looking for oil.

124
00:09:53,009 --> 00:09:54,626
There was a distress signal and then

125
00:09:54,638 --> 00:09:56,221
all communication just shut down.

126
00:09:56,304 --> 00:09:58,409
We're the only country in position to

127
00:09:58,421 --> 00:10:00,767
rescue. So, 20 minutes ago, we offered.

128
00:10:00,850 --> 00:10:03,561
- They declined?
- And denied.

129
00:10:03,645 --> 00:10:06,398
Petrov does not want us
to see what's on board.

130
00:10:08,191 --> 00:10:10,414
I understand this is
a difficult decision

131
00:10:10,439 --> 00:10:12,344
but there are human lives at stake,

132
00:10:12,404 --> 00:10:13,905
and if help arrives,

133
00:10:13,988 --> 00:10:16,658
- Petrov will be forced to take it.
- Okay.

134
00:10:16,741 --> 00:10:19,827
Well, thank you. I need to speak
with President Petrov directly.

135
00:10:22,330 --> 00:10:25,708
A rescue at sea and a
chance to embarrass us?

136
00:10:28,628 --> 00:10:31,381
Just before your second Election Day?

137
00:10:36,261 --> 00:10:38,846
Sounds like I would be helping you, hmm?

138
00:10:38,930 --> 00:10:41,766
The boat sinks, your crew dies.

139
00:10:41,849 --> 00:10:43,601
How is that going to look at home?

140
00:10:43,685 --> 00:10:47,230
<i>I tell my people how things
look, and that's how they look.</i>

141
00:10:48,273 --> 00:10:49,982
<i>I want to show you something.</i>

142
00:10:50,733 --> 00:10:54,111
Wait a minute. Hmm.

143
00:10:54,196 --> 00:10:57,657
<i>- There. Do you like my face?</i>
- A beard doesn't suit you.

144
00:11:00,618 --> 00:11:03,663
I wonder how your citizens would
feel about what you've done.

145
00:11:03,746 --> 00:11:06,541
I could return your
crew to Russia, Viktor.

146
00:11:06,624 --> 00:11:08,710
You could return Aidan Macallan.

147
00:11:08,793 --> 00:11:12,214
Ah, I don't like to
quantify human worth...

148
00:11:14,299 --> 00:11:16,050
but Macallan's is higher.

149
00:11:17,427 --> 00:11:20,096
Social media is a powerful tool.

150
00:11:20,179 --> 00:11:22,890
<i>If there is any rescue attempt
by the United States,</i>

151
00:11:22,974 --> 00:11:26,394
<i>I will tell the people of Ohio
exactly how you look to them.</i>

152
00:11:26,478 --> 00:11:29,647
<i>Exactly how you disrupted
their state's vote.</i>

153
00:11:32,734 --> 00:11:34,486
<i>And they will believe me.</i>

154
00:11:36,863 --> 00:11:38,448
Oppenheimer was a member.

155
00:11:38,531 --> 00:11:39,574
That tree right over there,

156
00:11:39,657 --> 00:11:42,827
right under that tree,
Manhattan Project was born.

157
00:11:42,910 --> 00:11:46,080
And right under this one,
I'm relieving myself.

158
00:11:47,832 --> 00:11:50,835
I hope you're not equating
me to the atom bomb.

159
00:11:52,962 --> 00:11:55,923
What do you want? Cabinet position?

160
00:11:56,007 --> 00:11:57,759
- Frank Underwood...
- Judicial appointments?

161
00:11:57,842 --> 00:12:00,303
- Legislative support?
- Ah, buzzing bees, buzzing bees.

162
00:12:00,387 --> 00:12:03,431
Oh, right. Because the Manhattan
Project had absolutely nothing to do

163
00:12:03,515 --> 00:12:06,142
- with business or politics.
- I want you to breathe.

164
00:12:06,893 --> 00:12:11,188
I want you to look how the
moonlight cuts through the trees.

165
00:12:13,149 --> 00:12:16,694
Take this time, Francis, to
know your other attendees.

166
00:12:16,778 --> 00:12:19,614
Think about what kind of
president you really want to be.

167
00:12:19,697 --> 00:12:21,908
What am I supposed to do with that?

168
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:24,702
Just...

169
00:12:26,329 --> 00:12:27,955
Oh, is that what you told the general?

170
00:12:28,665 --> 00:12:30,249
Insubordination.

171
00:12:30,875 --> 00:12:32,251
Clandestine coup.

172
00:12:32,960 --> 00:12:34,837
Oh, yes, that's the stuff
of real leadership.

173
00:12:34,921 --> 00:12:36,819
People with money like uniforms.

174
00:12:36,831 --> 00:12:38,966
Mark, just tell me what they want.

175
00:12:43,930 --> 00:12:44,931
They're waiting for you.

176
00:13:07,036 --> 00:13:08,955
You're up early, Mr. President.

177
00:13:10,081 --> 00:13:11,749
Always.

178
00:13:11,833 --> 00:13:17,589
Are you hungry? I have, uh, eggs,
and orange juice, mountain oysters.

179
00:13:19,674 --> 00:13:22,009
You mean bull's testicles, don't you?

180
00:13:22,093 --> 00:13:24,721
I prefer to call things what they are.

181
00:13:24,804 --> 00:13:28,933
Ah. This is unexpected.

182
00:13:30,059 --> 00:13:33,938
I thought you'd stay out there
campaigning till your dying breath.

183
00:13:34,021 --> 00:13:36,524
What, and leave you out here
all alone in the wilderness

184
00:13:36,608 --> 00:13:38,776
to make all these important decisions?

185
00:13:41,320 --> 00:13:44,115
You've done well by me, Raymond.
I'd like your support.

186
00:13:44,198 --> 00:13:45,658
I've done well by your wife.

187
00:13:45,742 --> 00:13:50,121
Who happens, at this moment,
to rule the White House.

188
00:13:50,204 --> 00:13:54,083
Tell me you're not peddling
influence with your wife?

189
00:13:54,166 --> 00:13:58,295
Who, you mean the current acting
President of the United States?

190
00:13:58,380 --> 00:14:00,131
For the next two weeks.

191
00:14:00,923 --> 00:14:04,636
A little restraint? Now, that...
that would be unexpected.

192
00:14:04,719 --> 00:14:07,013
How do you like your eggs?

193
00:14:07,096 --> 00:14:09,265
In the afternoon.

194
00:14:09,348 --> 00:14:10,475
Good morning, Mr. President.

195
00:14:10,558 --> 00:14:13,395
Oh, no presidents here at Elysian
Fields, isn't that right?

196
00:14:14,562 --> 00:14:18,274
Only former ones... or future.

197
00:14:19,692 --> 00:14:24,489
Well, I won't call this politics, then.
Just current affairs.

198
00:14:24,572 --> 00:14:26,866
We had a real chance to get Ahmadi.

199
00:14:27,450 --> 00:14:28,701
Our campaign was briefed.

200
00:14:29,536 --> 00:14:31,746
I think a little more than just briefed.

201
00:14:32,830 --> 00:14:35,041
Do you have an extra
pair of socks, Raymond?

202
00:14:35,124 --> 00:14:36,709
After 30 years of military service,

203
00:14:36,793 --> 00:14:38,795
you'd think I'd arrive prepared.

204
00:14:38,878 --> 00:14:41,923
Oh, I'm sure someone around
here could find you a pair.

205
00:14:43,132 --> 00:14:45,468
- Under my bunk.
- Thank you.

206
00:14:50,765 --> 00:14:52,308
He's a good man.

207
00:14:53,685 --> 00:14:58,523
Hard to find anybody who
distrusts or dislikes him.

208
00:14:58,606 --> 00:14:59,691
Hmm.

209
00:15:02,735 --> 00:15:04,361
So do you want eggs or not?

210
00:15:15,957 --> 00:15:17,915
With this rate of tariff, I'm confident

211
00:15:17,927 --> 00:15:19,544
it'll have a mitigating effect

212
00:15:19,627 --> 00:15:21,212
on the anti-dumping cases.

213
00:15:22,129 --> 00:15:23,881
I think this is going to work.

214
00:15:23,965 --> 00:15:26,551
I'll need to run this by my State
Councilor for Foreign Affairs.

215
00:15:26,634 --> 00:15:29,554
I think this is a very good day
for both of our countries.

216
00:15:29,637 --> 00:15:30,805
Yes, it is.

217
00:15:31,931 --> 00:15:33,390
There's an American on the boat.

218
00:15:34,976 --> 00:15:37,186
The Russian research vessel.

219
00:15:37,269 --> 00:15:40,356
- And how do you know that?
- Mr. Xu told me before you came in.

220
00:15:43,067 --> 00:15:45,236
I will need proof of that fact.

221
00:15:45,319 --> 00:15:47,484
Of course, but we are only telling you

222
00:15:47,509 --> 00:15:50,015
because we know you'll
want to rescue him.

223
00:15:50,074 --> 00:15:53,453
And China is willing to
help in any way we can.

224
00:15:55,287 --> 00:15:59,917
I'm just curious, Mr. Xu, why are
you so focused on this situation?

225
00:16:01,586 --> 00:16:03,630
For humanitarian reasons.

226
00:16:07,174 --> 00:16:11,721
Thank you for your time and
cooperation, Madam Acting President.

227
00:16:28,029 --> 00:16:29,280
It's contingent.

228
00:16:30,532 --> 00:16:33,117
The minister won't proceed
with our tariff deal

229
00:16:33,200 --> 00:16:36,245
if we don't give him assurances
that the rescue is underway.

230
00:16:36,328 --> 00:16:39,415
Why do the Chinese care so much
about what's on this Russian boat?

231
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:43,085
I don't know. I was
just informed myself.

232
00:16:43,169 --> 00:16:44,504
Are they working together?

233
00:16:44,587 --> 00:16:46,422
Again, I don't know.

234
00:16:48,299 --> 00:16:50,134
Are you working with them?

235
00:16:51,135 --> 00:16:53,220
I work with everybody.

236
00:16:53,304 --> 00:16:56,683
But that's beside the point.
There is an American on board.

237
00:16:56,766 --> 00:16:58,184
And you believe that?

238
00:16:58,851 --> 00:17:01,688
Well, you wouldn't want
to make the wrong call.

239
00:17:05,775 --> 00:17:07,985
Bull's balls.

240
00:17:08,069 --> 00:17:10,112
A giant crow.

241
00:17:10,196 --> 00:17:13,866
And billionaires
chanting, "Set me free."

242
00:17:13,950 --> 00:17:17,453
Well, this place says everything
I feel, but can't express.

243
00:17:21,082 --> 00:17:23,167
He can't be your friend.

244
00:17:23,793 --> 00:17:25,837
Tusk doesn't have any friends.

245
00:17:27,714 --> 00:17:29,591
He's, uh, an associate.

246
00:17:30,550 --> 00:17:32,594
- Really?
- Mmm.

247
00:17:32,677 --> 00:17:34,929
And how does he associate with you?

248
00:17:38,891 --> 00:17:41,435
Oh, to hell with the buzzing bees.
No one else is heeding it.

249
00:17:41,518 --> 00:17:45,106
A network president, a telecom CEO,
the vice presidential candidate.

250
00:17:45,189 --> 00:17:47,149
What do you think they're
all talking about,

251
00:17:47,233 --> 00:17:49,944
- the last book they read?
- What are you reading?

252
00:17:52,363 --> 00:17:53,615
Your face...

253
00:17:54,531 --> 00:17:57,159
which tells me how much you
don't want to be here.

254
00:18:00,246 --> 00:18:02,999
Mr. Tusk was a major donor
on Will's campaign.

255
00:18:03,082 --> 00:18:06,502
Yes, so it's interesting
that he wasn't invited.

256
00:18:08,713 --> 00:18:10,923
We're partnering on a project.

257
00:18:11,007 --> 00:18:14,802
Large-scale data storage
centers in the desert.

258
00:18:14,886 --> 00:18:16,470
Solar-powered.

259
00:18:19,015 --> 00:18:20,725
I need to get ready for my talk.

260
00:18:20,808 --> 00:18:23,728
"The Life After: What We Leave Behind."

261
00:18:25,062 --> 00:18:26,063
Hope you'll be there.

262
00:18:32,069 --> 00:18:33,112
Smell that.

263
00:18:34,321 --> 00:18:35,531
Cohiba.

264
00:18:36,866 --> 00:18:38,284
I know you're a fan.

265
00:18:39,410 --> 00:18:42,371
Even though I don't smoke
anymore, doesn't mean I can't...

266
00:18:42,454 --> 00:18:44,540
Who called this weekend's
gathering? You?

267
00:18:44,624 --> 00:18:47,168
No. Tusk did.

268
00:18:49,420 --> 00:18:51,547
- Why?
- Hmm.

269
00:18:52,965 --> 00:18:54,511
We think the Chinese are

270
00:18:54,523 --> 00:18:56,260
right. There is an American on board.

271
00:18:56,343 --> 00:18:59,722
This is Thad Peterson.
Retired two years ago.

272
00:18:59,806 --> 00:19:01,748
Director of geophysics
research for some

273
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:03,475
of the country's top oil companies.

274
00:19:03,559 --> 00:19:05,394
We strongly suspect
he's on board the boat.

275
00:19:05,477 --> 00:19:07,104
Working with the Russians.

276
00:19:09,398 --> 00:19:11,275
Where's the proof?

277
00:19:11,358 --> 00:19:13,277
This was sent from a Russian
sailor's cell phone

278
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:15,237
before communications were cut off.

279
00:19:18,658 --> 00:19:21,285
<i>Get on the radio and call for help!</i>

280
00:19:22,244 --> 00:19:24,038
His wife confirmed that this is

281
00:19:24,050 --> 00:19:25,998
Thad Peterson's voice on the recording.

282
00:19:26,082 --> 00:19:30,795
In 24, 36 hours tops, that boat's gone.

283
00:19:31,378 --> 00:19:34,716
Yes, but that doesn't prove that
Thad Peterson was on the ship.

284
00:19:37,719 --> 00:19:40,471
But, ma'am... he is an American...

285
00:19:40,554 --> 00:19:43,599
No, we can't be sure until we
have a passenger manifest.

286
00:19:44,391 --> 00:19:45,893
Let's reconvene when you know more.

287
00:19:53,400 --> 00:19:56,653
- What is it, Cathy?
- Your decision not to intervene.

288
00:19:57,739 --> 00:19:59,376
If an American is working illegally,

289
00:19:59,388 --> 00:20:00,992
he will not be on the manifest...

290
00:20:01,075 --> 00:20:04,203
I haven't made a decision. I'm
waiting for more information.

291
00:20:05,204 --> 00:20:07,707
This is about Aidan Macallan.

292
00:20:09,083 --> 00:20:13,254
I am very confident China is lying
about an American being on board.

293
00:20:13,337 --> 00:20:15,506
There is something on
that boat they want

294
00:20:15,589 --> 00:20:17,967
and I can't proceed until
I know what it is.

295
00:20:18,050 --> 00:20:21,262
They say they're motivated
by saving human lives.

296
00:20:21,345 --> 00:20:22,930
But that's not what's driving them.

297
00:20:23,848 --> 00:20:26,380
And I just find it so frustrating when

298
00:20:26,405 --> 00:20:29,335
human life is treated
with such disregard.

299
00:20:30,646 --> 00:20:31,898
<i>We're sorry.</i>

300
00:20:31,981 --> 00:20:35,943
The proverbial walk
in the woods is not for me.

301
00:20:36,027 --> 00:20:38,738
Navel-gazing on some forest path.

302
00:20:38,821 --> 00:20:42,533
Contemplating mortality. Immortality.

303
00:20:42,616 --> 00:20:45,953
No. History is earned.

304
00:20:46,037 --> 00:20:48,455
You win the day, and then
you win the day after,

305
00:20:48,539 --> 00:20:51,000
and you do the same thing
every day until you're dead.

306
00:20:51,083 --> 00:20:52,855
And you're remembered not for the

307
00:20:52,867 --> 00:20:54,921
winning, but because you never lost.

308
00:20:56,130 --> 00:20:57,173
Now...

309
00:21:02,094 --> 00:21:03,262
Doug?

310
00:21:04,305 --> 00:21:05,722
Doug, can you hear me?

311
00:21:06,390 --> 00:21:08,350
I need some leverage out here right now.

312
00:21:08,976 --> 00:21:11,771
- What's going on?
<i>- Conway is finished.</i>

313
00:21:11,854 --> 00:21:12,897
<i>If they win,</i>

314
00:21:12,980 --> 00:21:14,523
Brockhart will do all
the heavy lifting.

315
00:21:14,535 --> 00:21:15,900
He'll essentially be the president.

316
00:21:15,983 --> 00:21:17,526
<i>We should have used the oppo.</i>

317
00:21:17,609 --> 00:21:20,446
<i>Listen, Brockhart is Tusk's pick.</i>

318
00:21:20,529 --> 00:21:23,365
<i>I need to find a way to force
that old man to support me.</i>

319
00:21:23,449 --> 00:21:26,285
- Sir? Mr. President?
<i>- Do you hear me?</i>

320
00:21:32,499 --> 00:21:34,543
I'm here.

321
00:21:34,626 --> 00:21:38,589
Tusk has some deal with Pollyhop. I
need to find a way to stop that.

322
00:21:38,672 --> 00:21:40,174
An executive order...

323
00:21:40,257 --> 00:21:41,968
Ah!

324
00:21:45,721 --> 00:21:46,722
<i>Sir?</i>

325
00:21:49,934 --> 00:21:51,352
A little restraint...

326
00:21:52,895 --> 00:21:54,521
would be unexpected.

327
00:21:57,900 --> 00:21:59,276
<i>Mr. President?</i>

328
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:01,070
- Doug.
<i>- Sir?</i>

329
00:22:01,153 --> 00:22:02,321
<i>Don't do anything.</i>

330
00:22:03,239 --> 00:22:04,656
I don't understand.

331
00:22:04,740 --> 00:22:06,951
<i>Don't do anything until
you hear from me.</i>

332
00:22:07,034 --> 00:22:10,454
- Sir, what about the Pollyhop deal?
<i>- Do you hear me?</i>

333
00:22:10,537 --> 00:22:12,832
<i>- Don't do anything...</i>
- Mr. President, I...

334
00:22:24,218 --> 00:22:25,991
Have you tried calling him back?

335
00:22:26,016 --> 00:22:27,787
There's no answer. What should we do?

336
00:22:27,846 --> 00:22:30,599
Nothing. That's what the president
said, so that's what we'll do.

337
00:22:30,682 --> 00:22:33,060
- Should we have his team pull him out?
- No. We wait.

338
00:22:34,061 --> 00:22:37,066
Ma'am... if we don't handle
this rescue correctly...

339
00:22:37,231 --> 00:22:37,925
Doug.

340
00:22:37,950 --> 00:22:40,328
Let me find a way to get the
president on the phone with Petrov.

341
00:22:40,609 --> 00:22:42,653
No. Francis doesn't
need this distraction.

342
00:22:42,736 --> 00:22:43,779
I disagree.

343
00:22:44,280 --> 00:22:45,281
I know you do.

344
00:22:46,282 --> 00:22:49,368
They say we're only
remembered for three generations.

345
00:22:49,451 --> 00:22:52,871
What would one have to
do not to be forgotten?

346
00:22:54,151 --> 00:22:56,112
Young Benjamin here knows what's coming.

347
00:22:57,293 --> 00:22:59,461
Who cares if he's never
heard of Johnny Carson?

348
00:23:01,672 --> 00:23:06,093
Poor old Tusk. He wears that game
face, but he doesn't fool me.

349
00:23:06,177 --> 00:23:10,139
The future terrifies him
because he knows it's short.

350
00:23:10,222 --> 00:23:12,766
For the record, I have
heard of Johnny Carson.

351
00:23:12,849 --> 00:23:13,684
Ah.

352
00:23:13,767 --> 00:23:15,436
He tucked my
parents into bed every night.

353
00:23:20,607 --> 00:23:22,982
I've been given special permission to

354
00:23:22,994 --> 00:23:25,446
show you what we've been working on.

355
00:23:25,529 --> 00:23:30,242
Imagine the possibility of your
brain, your consciousness,

356
00:23:30,326 --> 00:23:32,453
stored digitally in real time.

357
00:23:32,536 --> 00:23:34,663
I mean, what is immortality?

358
00:23:34,746 --> 00:23:38,292
In time, even Grant's
vision will get old.

359
00:23:38,375 --> 00:23:42,296
It will happen, as it
does for all of us.

360
00:23:42,379 --> 00:23:44,590
Now this is still in the early stages,

361
00:23:44,602 --> 00:23:46,883
but we've been collecting and storing

362
00:23:46,968 --> 00:23:50,679
Raymond's memories,
thoughts, quirks and tics.

363
00:23:51,597 --> 00:23:54,475
This is a mock-up. But eventually...

364
00:23:58,312 --> 00:24:03,317
this Raymond... will feel as real
as the one standing before you.

365
00:24:04,235 --> 00:24:07,363
I like dirt and rocks...

366
00:24:09,698 --> 00:24:10,699
and facts.

367
00:24:12,701 --> 00:24:14,578
No argument here.

368
00:24:14,661 --> 00:24:18,040
I trust only what I can hold in
my hand or see on my horizon.

369
00:24:18,124 --> 00:24:21,627
Well, but you can't just sit
back and admire the view.

370
00:24:21,710 --> 00:24:25,339
I've learned you've got to
grab the present by the balls.

371
00:24:25,422 --> 00:24:27,424
That's where the American people live.

372
00:24:27,508 --> 00:24:30,511
You sound more like a salesman
than a president, Mr. President.

373
00:24:30,594 --> 00:24:33,305
Well, if we're Americans,
then we are salesmen, right?

374
00:24:33,389 --> 00:24:37,684
That's our birthright. Cars and
phones and a gallon of gas.

375
00:24:37,768 --> 00:24:39,436
That's not how I see America.

376
00:24:40,354 --> 00:24:41,897
I'm a patriot. I serve.

377
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:45,401
Well, then, we salute you, General.
But you serve to what purpose?

378
00:24:46,027 --> 00:24:48,237
So the rest of America can sell.

379
00:24:48,320 --> 00:24:50,906
I mean, you and I, right
now, this weekend,

380
00:24:50,989 --> 00:24:53,575
we're here at this camp
selling ourselves.

381
00:24:54,993 --> 00:24:57,663
Character speaks for itself.

382
00:24:57,746 --> 00:24:59,415
People want a president they can trust.

383
00:24:59,498 --> 00:25:02,126
No, they don't. They want
a president they know.

384
00:25:03,044 --> 00:25:04,670
And I know them.

385
00:25:05,337 --> 00:25:08,632
The only future I'm interested
in is the next four years.

386
00:25:08,715 --> 00:25:11,677
So, yes, I'm selling tomorrow
and the day after that

387
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:13,304
and the day after that.

388
00:25:13,387 --> 00:25:16,223
I'm selling a boy who used
to live on a peach farm

389
00:25:16,307 --> 00:25:18,350
who now lives in the White House.

390
00:25:18,434 --> 00:25:24,398
I'm selling a tomorrow that you
can see and touch and feel.

391
00:25:25,107 --> 00:25:29,111
Now, you take that away, and I don't
know what this country has left.

392
00:25:29,195 --> 00:25:31,613
Except a group of grown men

393
00:25:31,697 --> 00:25:35,659
standing around in a circle,
all staring at a small screen.

394
00:25:35,742 --> 00:25:37,911
Looking in instead of looking out.

395
00:25:37,994 --> 00:25:41,290
Heck, give me a peck of
dirt any day of the week.

396
00:25:43,041 --> 00:25:45,919
Hear, hear. To a peck of dirt.

397
00:25:46,002 --> 00:25:48,004
- Hear, hear.
- I will drink to that,

398
00:25:48,089 --> 00:25:52,968
but I will also drink to the
general, a good servant.

399
00:25:53,051 --> 00:25:54,511
To the general.

400
00:25:56,763 --> 00:26:00,351
I promise you, this deal
is on the verge of falling apart.

401
00:26:00,434 --> 00:26:02,561
Right before your revote.

402
00:26:02,644 --> 00:26:04,896
I... I'm only the messenger, by the way.

403
00:26:06,732 --> 00:26:08,150
We can't let this deal fall apart.

404
00:26:08,234 --> 00:26:11,820
Agreed. But the rescue has
to happen immediately.

405
00:26:12,404 --> 00:26:14,740
You seem personally invested in this.

406
00:26:14,823 --> 00:26:18,119
Oh, no, no. I'm just
looking for a solution.

407
00:26:21,413 --> 00:26:22,623
I had a thought.

408
00:26:24,082 --> 00:26:26,752
I would be willing to
allow the Chinese sailors

409
00:26:26,835 --> 00:26:30,964
that are stationed on King George
Island to board our icebreaker.

410
00:26:31,047 --> 00:26:33,384
Then, purely for the in-house cameras,

411
00:26:33,467 --> 00:26:36,387
we let it appear as though
the Chinese led the rescue.

412
00:26:36,470 --> 00:26:38,472
Now, of course, the whole
time, the Americans

413
00:26:38,555 --> 00:26:40,224
would remain in charge of the boat,

414
00:26:40,307 --> 00:26:43,185
but the Russians don't need
to know our involvement.

415
00:26:43,269 --> 00:26:45,771
I imagine that would be acceptable.

416
00:26:47,523 --> 00:26:50,066
I'll relay this scenario to Mr.
Xu immediately.

417
00:26:50,151 --> 00:26:53,529
But I'd also like to round
up the tariff to 20%.

418
00:26:54,863 --> 00:26:57,366
We have a firm deal at 19.

419
00:26:57,449 --> 00:27:00,702
I know. But suddenly, it just
doesn't seem high enough.

420
00:27:02,579 --> 00:27:04,456
I think they'll be okay with it.

421
00:27:06,082 --> 00:27:09,044
Fingers crossed. I'll do my best.

422
00:27:09,127 --> 00:27:13,089
- That went rather well, I think.
- It did. It went very well.

423
00:27:13,174 --> 00:27:14,925
You've exceeded everyone's expectations,

424
00:27:15,008 --> 00:27:17,428
the way you handled yourself
all weekend, but...

425
00:27:22,558 --> 00:27:25,143
Brockhart's someone they can predict.

426
00:27:25,227 --> 00:27:28,939
Don't get ahead of yourself.
This is not over.

427
00:27:29,022 --> 00:27:31,483
What are you gonna do, Francis?
Are you gonna use the oppo?

428
00:27:31,567 --> 00:27:34,403
Well, it seems to have
worried you enough.

429
00:27:34,486 --> 00:27:36,655
He's still only the vice
presidential candidate.

430
00:27:36,738 --> 00:27:39,241
I'm leaving.

431
00:27:39,325 --> 00:27:40,331
You don't have to go.

432
00:27:40,356 --> 00:27:42,560
No, I know. I don't
have to do anything.

433
00:27:44,371 --> 00:27:46,465
Do you remember when we first met? You

434
00:27:46,477 --> 00:27:48,417
almost joined my second campaign.

435
00:27:48,500 --> 00:27:51,044
We sat down at that little
greasy spoon in Gaffney.

436
00:27:51,127 --> 00:27:53,797
Yeah, if you'd signed me on, you
would have won by a landslide.

437
00:27:53,880 --> 00:27:57,301
I won anyway, because I never lose.

438
00:27:57,968 --> 00:28:00,387
You know why I didn't
hire you back then?

439
00:28:00,471 --> 00:28:02,264
Because when you went
to pick up the check,

440
00:28:02,348 --> 00:28:04,850
you told me I didn't have to pay you.

441
00:28:04,933 --> 00:28:08,937
That you would work for free.
I don't trust that.

442
00:28:09,020 --> 00:28:11,022
Because no one works for free.

443
00:28:13,609 --> 00:28:17,321
<i>Madam President,
I'm afraid I can't agree to 20%.</i>

444
00:28:17,404 --> 00:28:20,324
<i>- The economic implications...</i>
- We're at an impasse.

445
00:28:21,325 --> 00:28:23,577
<i>I'm willing to keep the
conversation going,</i>

446
00:28:23,660 --> 00:28:26,788
<i>- but in the meantime...</i>
- We'll do nothing until you agree.

447
00:28:27,831 --> 00:28:30,000
<i>Madam President, people will die.</i>

448
00:28:30,083 --> 00:28:32,794
Which is why I'm surprised
you won't agree to my terms.

449
00:28:32,878 --> 00:28:34,755
<i>I can't agree to the terms
without the approval</i>

450
00:28:34,838 --> 00:28:37,424
<i>of my State Councillor
for Foreign Affairs.</i>

451
00:28:40,010 --> 00:28:43,889
There are 112 people aboard the vessel.

452
00:28:43,972 --> 00:28:46,225
113 if you count Mr. Peterson.

453
00:28:47,434 --> 00:28:51,021
Now, it will go under in 20 to 32 hours.

454
00:28:51,104 --> 00:28:53,282
The temperature of the Southern Ocean

455
00:28:53,294 --> 00:28:55,484
right now is 28 degrees Fahrenheit.

456
00:28:55,567 --> 00:29:01,282
A body will freeze to death within
45 minutes in water that cold.

457
00:29:01,365 --> 00:29:04,743
And, of course, one loses the
ability to swim long before that.

458
00:29:08,539 --> 00:29:10,499
Minister Xu, are you still there?

459
00:29:13,252 --> 00:29:16,880
Collaborating with President
Petrov is always a mistake.

460
00:29:16,963 --> 00:29:19,841
The 20% tariff will help
you keep that in mind.

461
00:29:20,884 --> 00:29:22,177
I'm waiting.

462
00:29:23,262 --> 00:29:25,389
It's up to you if they live or die.

463
00:29:33,730 --> 00:29:35,982
- The crew?
- All rescued.

464
00:29:37,984 --> 00:29:38,854
And Thad Peterson?

465
00:29:38,879 --> 00:29:41,053
You were right. The Chinese were lying.

466
00:29:41,112 --> 00:29:43,657
He's the only one still unaccounted for.

467
00:29:45,784 --> 00:29:48,912
The admiral says there's still
a chance to save the boat.

468
00:29:53,083 --> 00:29:54,585
Let it go down.

469
00:29:55,627 --> 00:29:57,345
And whatever Petrov has on it?

470
00:29:57,357 --> 00:29:59,465
We're doing the right thing, Cathy.

471
00:30:00,841 --> 00:30:03,344
<i>You got what you wanted, Viktor.</i>

472
00:30:03,427 --> 00:30:06,513
<i>The boat is gone. Now let's get
back to where we left off.</i>

473
00:30:07,013 --> 00:30:10,684
What will it take to get Aidan
Macallan back on American soil?

474
00:30:14,229 --> 00:30:15,564
He likes it here.

475
00:30:29,870 --> 00:30:31,705
Leaving, Mr. President?

476
00:30:35,083 --> 00:30:37,168
You can put that in the car, thank you.

477
00:30:39,338 --> 00:30:42,257
- Are you all right?
- Better now.

478
00:30:43,550 --> 00:30:45,218
Are you drunk?

479
00:30:45,301 --> 00:30:48,138
- You want a pull?
- No.

480
00:30:49,640 --> 00:30:51,224
I hate this place.

481
00:30:51,808 --> 00:30:52,976
These men.

482
00:30:54,269 --> 00:30:56,855
Their privilege, their presumption.

483
00:30:58,064 --> 00:31:00,025
The deal with Tusk was a mistake.

484
00:31:01,151 --> 00:31:02,986
Do you know...

485
00:31:03,069 --> 00:31:06,322
Raymond wants to be frozen?

486
00:31:06,407 --> 00:31:09,451
Put on ice until a later date.

487
00:31:09,535 --> 00:31:13,079
- I have to get going.
- Will Conway's my friend. But...

488
00:31:13,163 --> 00:31:16,124
this won't end well for him if he wins.

489
00:31:16,207 --> 00:31:19,169
You take care of yourself, Ben.

490
00:31:19,753 --> 00:31:21,547
They have a recording of him.

491
00:31:24,383 --> 00:31:26,510
I'll be right there in a minute.

492
00:31:37,187 --> 00:31:38,897
You have to promise me

493
00:31:38,980 --> 00:31:42,401
Will Conway never finds
out this came from me.

494
00:31:59,125 --> 00:32:01,628
<i>You know
I'm gonna be the president, right?</i>

495
00:32:01,712 --> 00:32:04,339
<i>- I hope so, sir.
- You hope so?</i>

496
00:32:04,423 --> 00:32:06,467
<i>I just mean I'm pulling for you, sir.</i>

497
00:32:06,550 --> 00:32:07,843
<i>I'm gonna be the president,</i>

498
00:32:07,926 --> 00:32:10,471
<i>and you're gonna flip me those
motherfucking controls...</i>

499
00:32:10,554 --> 00:32:11,555
<i>Sir!</i>

500
00:32:11,638 --> 00:32:13,890
I think this must have been
recorded by the pilot.

501
00:32:13,974 --> 00:32:16,101
<i>We should go to MSNBC and CNN first.</i>

502
00:32:16,184 --> 00:32:18,479
- <i>We can go wide.</i>
- No, let's wait.

503
00:32:20,564 --> 00:32:23,108
Francis, you heard what I heard.

504
00:32:23,734 --> 00:32:25,611
And it'll be just as good tomorrow.

505
00:32:25,694 --> 00:32:28,071
Have Doyle confirm it. I'll
see you in the morning.

506
00:32:32,743 --> 00:32:35,203
Why not do it now?

507
00:32:35,787 --> 00:32:37,706
We need to wait for the president.

508
00:32:39,082 --> 00:32:40,584
<i>Stories are still swirling</i>

509
00:32:40,667 --> 00:32:43,962
<i>around the events leading up
to a daring Chinese-led rescue</i>

510
00:32:44,045 --> 00:32:45,547
<i>of the crew of a Russian research ship</i>

511
00:32:45,631 --> 00:32:47,507
<i>stuck in ice for almost four days off</i>

512
00:32:47,519 --> 00:32:49,510
<i>the coast of the Antarctic Peninsula.</i>

513
00:32:49,593 --> 00:32:52,387
<i>Although the ship was lost,
every crew member was saved.</i>

514
00:32:52,471 --> 00:32:53,972
<i>Russian President Viktor Petrov</i>

515
00:32:54,055 --> 00:32:57,559
<i>expressed his gratitude to the
Chinese government this morning.</i>

516
00:32:59,728 --> 00:33:02,313
<i>Exhaustion must be
catching up with the candidates,</i>

517
00:33:02,397 --> 00:33:05,776
<i>as President Underwood and his
running mate took the weekend off.</i>

518
00:33:05,859 --> 00:33:09,237
<i>General Brockhart was nowhere to be
seen, while only Governor Conway</i>

519
00:33:09,320 --> 00:33:10,781
<i>- made a pair of quick appearances...</i>
- Hey.

520
00:33:10,864 --> 00:33:12,407
<i>...in the Cleveland area.</i>

521
00:33:16,537 --> 00:33:18,364
That tap water is gonna kill you.

522
00:33:18,376 --> 00:33:20,582
There are worse ways to go.

523
00:33:23,794 --> 00:33:24,795
I have a confession.

524
00:33:28,882 --> 00:33:29,966
What'd you do?

525
00:33:33,094 --> 00:33:37,683
I've had somebody watch you
sometimes when you go to New York.

526
00:33:42,938 --> 00:33:44,397
Her name's Karen.

527
00:33:48,569 --> 00:33:49,778
Who is she?

528
00:33:52,405 --> 00:33:54,365
I don't know you get to ask that.

529
00:33:57,493 --> 00:33:58,787
She was my teacher.

530
00:34:00,121 --> 00:34:03,542
Theology, among other things.

531
00:34:06,461 --> 00:34:11,174
I don't know why I did that. It's not...
about jealousy.

532
00:34:13,259 --> 00:34:15,136
She saved my life, actually.

533
00:34:16,805 --> 00:34:17,889
I'm glad.

534
00:34:23,061 --> 00:34:25,063
Well, we won't be seeing
Karen for a while.

535
00:34:27,608 --> 00:34:31,277
She knows me. She could
see how I feel about you.

536
00:34:37,826 --> 00:34:39,786
Ma'am, an initialed agreement.

537
00:34:42,748 --> 00:34:45,876
What exactly was on that boat, Jane?

538
00:34:47,460 --> 00:34:49,520
Uh, the Chinese and the Russians had

539
00:34:49,545 --> 00:34:51,947
access to technology
they shouldn't have

540
00:34:52,007 --> 00:34:53,800
and didn't want to lose.

541
00:34:53,884 --> 00:34:55,343
And now they've lost it.

542
00:34:56,469 --> 00:34:58,096
What aren't you telling me?

543
00:35:01,057 --> 00:35:03,727
What did you want out of all of this?

544
00:35:04,645 --> 00:35:06,688
I told you what I wanted.

545
00:35:06,772 --> 00:35:09,440
I wanted that American off the boat.

546
00:35:12,903 --> 00:35:14,946
He was the technology.

547
00:35:21,202 --> 00:35:23,121
Here, give me that.

548
00:35:25,248 --> 00:35:26,457
Go.

549
00:35:39,971 --> 00:35:41,765
Tom Hammerschmidt.

550
00:35:45,977 --> 00:35:48,647
Detective, I got the drive.

551
00:35:50,523 --> 00:35:52,776
Uh, no, I just put it in.

552
00:35:54,402 --> 00:36:01,367
And... the time stamp says
November 3rd, 2013, 8:13 p.m.

553
00:36:01,451 --> 00:36:03,870
Hold on a second. You all right?

554
00:36:04,495 --> 00:36:07,290
Yeah. I just need to
get out for a minute.

555
00:36:11,502 --> 00:36:12,503
I'm here.

556
00:36:13,421 --> 00:36:16,098
I know. I know you're busy.

557
00:36:16,123 --> 00:36:18,408
And I can't thank you enough, I...

558
00:36:20,261 --> 00:36:21,722
Let me back you up a bit.

559
00:36:26,059 --> 00:36:28,687
Okay, so this is two
days before she died.

560
00:36:29,645 --> 00:36:32,482
The governor sounds unhinged.

561
00:36:32,565 --> 00:36:35,986
It bothers me, though. It came
too easy, don't you think?

562
00:36:36,069 --> 00:36:38,446
Yes, but if we use it, we'll win.

563
00:36:38,530 --> 00:36:40,782
I don't know, Claire. You
know these types of people.

564
00:36:40,865 --> 00:36:42,242
You should have been there.

565
00:36:42,325 --> 00:36:45,620
Why would they hand me... He
just handed me this. Why?

566
00:36:45,704 --> 00:36:47,789
When they've never wanted
me, not from the beginning,

567
00:36:47,873 --> 00:36:49,332
no matter what I do!

568
00:36:52,377 --> 00:36:55,213
- Fuck 'em.
- I intend to.

569
00:36:56,798 --> 00:36:58,759
And how do you intend to do that?

570
00:37:01,219 --> 00:37:02,888
I'm going back to work.

571
00:37:05,056 --> 00:37:06,767
I'm going back to work.

572
00:37:09,770 --> 00:37:11,730
We're glad you came.

573
00:37:11,813 --> 00:37:14,649
I hear those weekends can,
uh, take some recovery time.

574
00:37:15,984 --> 00:37:18,987
I must say, I didn't expect
to be back here so soon.

575
00:37:20,280 --> 00:37:23,449
Well, Mark, we asked you
back here because...

576
00:37:24,743 --> 00:37:27,913
we've come into possession
of a second audio recording

577
00:37:27,996 --> 00:37:30,165
of your candidate on an airplane.

578
00:37:31,082 --> 00:37:34,085
- He's been recorded saying...
- I know what he said.

579
00:37:34,169 --> 00:37:35,586
I was there.

580
00:37:41,717 --> 00:37:44,888
So, we were wondering if
we can discuss how...

581
00:37:46,431 --> 00:37:48,308
we might be able to work together.

582
00:37:48,391 --> 00:37:51,561
We can accomplish a great deal.
The three of us.

583
00:37:51,644 --> 00:37:53,063
Now, before you say anything,

584
00:37:53,146 --> 00:37:55,398
let me tell you how this
conversation's going to go.

585
00:37:55,481 --> 00:37:57,483
You're going to talk
about party loyalty,

586
00:37:57,567 --> 00:38:00,611
and I'm going to talk about
how weak your candidates are

587
00:38:00,695 --> 00:38:02,280
and that you really have no choice.

588
00:38:02,363 --> 00:38:06,451
And then you're going to say that
your ticket can still win Ohio.

589
00:38:06,534 --> 00:38:09,871
And I'm going to say, "No, they can't."

590
00:38:09,955 --> 00:38:13,499
But what's going to go
unsaid in this conversation

591
00:38:13,583 --> 00:38:15,751
is that Claire and I...

592
00:38:16,377 --> 00:38:20,590
are willing... to do anything.

593
00:38:21,842 --> 00:38:26,762
And we know... that
you are no different.

594
00:38:45,490 --> 00:38:48,701
Okay, I can start by setting up
some meetings for both of you.

595
00:38:48,785 --> 00:38:50,870
I can't do that publicly, of course.

596
00:38:51,913 --> 00:38:56,667
I suggest you release whatever
you have slowly. One at a time.

597
00:38:58,128 --> 00:38:59,795
Let's make this build.

598
00:39:01,256 --> 00:39:02,257
Once it gets out,

